in this video we'll talk about multiple  sclerosis or ms  sclerosis means scar so multiple  sclerosis means car over different  location of the central nervous system  the lesions are present  in the brain and in the spinal cord  multiple sclerosis is kind of like a  progressive demyelination  so in this case the white matter of the  brain and spinal cords get seriously  affected  and this progression of this disease is  really slow and often takes decades  and the demyelination is triggered by  immunological reactions there are  autoreactive  cells which can actually affect these  myelin sheath of the neurons which work  like a insulator  the structures  that are usually involved in  multiple sclerosis are actually the  components of cns  generally the optic and the oculomotor  nerves are actually affected also the  spinal nerve tract is affected but this  disorder does not affect the peripheral  nervous system that's the most important  part  so there are several symptoms which  includes vision problems which can be  understood because optic nerve tract is  affected lesions can develop there  numbness and tingling walking difficulty  dizziness weakness fatigue  cognitive dysfunction all of these  symptoms are associated with multiple  sclerosis  now let's talk about the key symptoms  this involves dysarthria which means  difficulty or unclear speech  because this  scars can also occur in the brain stem  and brain stem is crucial for  speech and look of speech and  communication that's why there could be  speech related problems  another symptom could be nystagmus that  means involuntary and rapid eye movement  and you know the optic  nerve tract and oculomotor nerve tract  is damaged in case of multiple sclerosis  probably this leads to this kind of  situation  this can lead to blurred vision double  vision or even other visual difficulties  lastly there could be intention tremors  that means  this there could be like tremors  developing due to lesions in the motor  pathways  all these three major symptoms are  collectively known as  the charcot's neurologic triad in  multiple sclerosis  now let's talk about the risk factors  associated with multiple sclerosis there  could be genetic as well as  environmental factors  so there are genetic factors such as  it has been shown that people who are  showing defects in their hla dr or hla  dq locus which are associated with class  2 mhc molecules  they show  more susceptibility towards developing  multiple sclerosis  also environmental factors such as viral  infections can also increase the risk of  multiple sclerosis or or are associated  with multiple sclerosis  let us talk about few more environmental  factors sunlight and vitamin d plays a  crucial role so overall you can  understand diet is important  and there is a overall factor that women  are more susceptible than man and this  is also because of a genetic factor  it has been shown the occurrence of  multiple sclerosis is much more in those  region  which has low sunlight and thereby  people have low vitamin d that means it  is lesser in the equator and it there  the incidence rise when we go away from  the equator and this heat map gives you  a quick idea  multiple sclerosis pathology is  characterized by confluent demyelited  areas  so this is how a healthy blend look like  and this is how a ms brain look like  in this case you can first see there are  cortical demyelination but cortical  demyelination takes some time and  happens in the advanced stage of this  disease initially the scars or the  lesions appears in the white matter and  these are detectable in mri imaging  and these scars are occurring due to the  defect or destruction of the myelin  sheath and the oligodendrocyte which  forms this myelin sheath  let's talk about the subtypes of  multiple sclerosis  so if we plot the percentage disability  in the y-axis and the age or the  timeline in the x-axis then the graphs  would look like this  this first type is known as relapsing  remitting multiple sclerosis  i'll come to it in a bit more details  the second type is known as secondary  progressive uh  multiple sclerosis then there is primary  progressive and progressive relapsing  all these are really important but the  most common one is the  rrms or relapsing remitting multiple  sclerosis here  there are bouts of autoimmune attacks  and that  is kind of spaced between months or  years so there are aggravation of this  disease  over time  and the secondary progressive multiple  sclerosis has a steady progression of  disability in the later half of this  particular disease  and in case of primary progressive  multiple sclerosis there is constant  attack on the myelin sheath so rapidly  rapidly the myelin sheaths are degraded  so disease progression is relatively  faster in this type of multiple  sclerosis  and progressive relapsing is even faster  so there are like continuous attack but  in bouts the attack becomes even more  severe  now let's talk about the immunology of  multiple sclerosis  so generally the t cells develop in the  thymus  but in the thymus there are mechanisms  known as central tolerance which  prevents the generation of autoreactive  t cells even in outside the thymus there  are peripheral tolerance which ensures  there are no autoreactive t cells which  could be harmful for the body but for  multiple sclerosis this tolerance  mechanism fails if you want to know more  about tolerance you can click on the  video in the i button  and that leads to many complications  there are too many autoreactive t cells  in the circulation they can ultimately  cross the blood brain barrier and can  enter the brain so the brain is no more  immune privileged and that is pretty  dangerous  inside the brain  there are several things that happens  so  in the myelin sheath there are antigens  such as myelin basic protein  proteolypic protein myelin  oligodendrocyte a glycoprotein all of  these antigens are detected by these  autoreactive t cells autoreactive t  cells actually  uh kind of get activated and  affect the myelin sheath  now there are infiltrating uh  antigen presenting cells which further  present these antigens to these t cells  now this aggravates the reaction  these t cell once get activated secrete  several other subtypes such as th1 or  th17  both of these t cells are highly  associated with inflammatory processes  they can secrete cytokines such as  interleukin 1 6 t  tnf alpha etc  now  overall  there are  th1 and th17 subtypes are associated  with multiple sclerosis and current  research shows there could be  one particular dominant population but  it's kind of debatable at this moment  anyway there could be other factors  which are affecting uh the disease  progression such as the regulatory t  cells regulatory t cells generally  prevent the activity of  any kind of authority or any kind of t  cells by producing the  cytokine interleukin-10 so it's kind of  like a speed breaker  but  in case of multiple sclerosis often it  was observed that the regulatory t cell  function is abrogated  that leads to further increase in  autoreactive t cell activity  that can be damaging for the brain  so let's talk about how can we diagnose  multiple sclerosis one of the option is  to look for clinically relevant  biomarkers and that can be done by  taking out the csf by lumber puncture  and analyzing the csf for several  biomarkers like myelin basic protein igm  oligoclonal bands  neural adhesion molecules and many other  factors  so these biomarkers can help us to  predict whether there is a  chance of developing multiple sclerosis  or not then the best way is to do by  magnet uh magnetic resonance imaging or  mri and this is the most useful test  diagnostic tool for multiple sclerosis  classically mri will show  lesions in the white matter in the brain  and that is pretty characteristic of  multiple sclerosis  currently there is no treatment for  multiple sclerosis but there are  supportive treatment and  immunosuppressants which can help  the patient to have a better lifestyle  or like it can reduce the pain  so it's kind of like a supportive  medicine but there is no absolute cure  for this particular disease  so i hope this video was useful if you  like this video give it a big thumbs up  you can get notes and flash cards  associated with many videos in my  facebook page the link is provided in  the description as usual don't forget to  like share and subscribe you can support  me on patreon or if you're indian viewer  then you can support me on beam upi app  my courses are also present in an  academy which is india's biggest online  learning platform you
 

